  <article class="doc">
    <header>
      <div class="tag">project 03 · security research &amp; reverse engineering</div>
      <h1>CS:CO Memory Tool — Process Inspection &amp; Game Security Research</h1>
      <div class="meta">
        <span>Python</span><span>C++</span><span>Windows API</span><span>reverse engineering</span>
        <span>memory</span><span>game security</span><span>pointer chains</span>
        <span>overlay</span><span>Cheat Engine</span><span>security research</span>
      </div>
    </header>

    <h2>Overview</h2>
    <p>This started as a question: what exactly is anti-cheat software doing? That question doesn't
      have a clean answer from the outside. To understand a defender, you have to understand the
      attack surface it's defending — and the only way to genuinely understand that surface is to
      build something that works against it. This tool is the result of that exercise.</p>
    <p>Over six iterations it grew from a basic memory reader into a multi-featured tool: a
      transparent fullscreen ESP overlay drawing entity boxes, health bars, and bone-accurate head
      circles; an aimbot with Bezier-smoothed angle writes and runtime recoil correction; a
      triggerbot that monitors a game-internal pointer chain for crosshair contact; and a radar
      hack that forces enemies visible on the minimap. All running concurrently across seven
      background threads, configured through a tabbed GUI.</p>
    <p>The final tool is written in Python using <code>pymem</code>. It did not start there. The
      first implementation was in C++ — a native Win32 binary built in Visual Studio, using the
      same <code>ReadProcessMemory</code> and <code>WriteProcessMemory</code> calls directly.
      That prototype ran into compilation errors, toolchain mismatches, and Windows Defender
      quarantining the binary before it could run. Those problems were not obstacles — they were
      lessons. The path from that first broken C++ compile to a working Python overlay is as much
      a part of this project as the finished code.</p>
    <p>The point was never the features. The point was the mental model they force you to build: a
      running process is not a black box. Its internal state is fully readable by anything with
      the right handle, its behaviour fully modifiable by anything with write access, and the only
      thing standing between an attacker and that access is the OS permission model and the
      software in the same privilege ring enforcing it. Building something that breaks a game is
      one of the fastest ways to understand what defending a process actually requires.</p>

    <!-- ============================================================ -->
    <h2>Prologue — The C++ Prototype: Where It Actually Started</h2>

    <p>The first version was not Python. It was a C++ Visual Studio project built around a
      handwritten <code>Memory</code> class that wrapped <code>OpenProcess</code>,
      <code>ReadProcessMemory</code>, and <code>WriteProcessMemory</code> in a clean templated
      interface. The interface looked like this:</p>
    <span class="label">memory class — c++ prototype</span>
    <pre>template &lt;typename T&gt;
constexpr const T Read(const std::uintptr_t&amp; address) const noexcept
{
    T value = { };
    ::ReadProcessMemory(processHandle,
        reinterpret_cast&lt;const void*&gt;(address), &amp;value, sizeof(T), NULL);
    return value;
}

template &lt;typename T&gt;
constexpr void Write(const std::uintptr_t&amp; address, const T&amp; value) const noexcept
{
    ::WriteProcessMemory(processHandle,
        reinterpret_cast&lt;void*&gt;(address), &amp;value, sizeof(T), NULL);
}</pre>
    <p>This is a cleaner interface than <code>pymem</code> in some ways — the template parameter
      makes the type explicit at the call site. The same approach appears in professional memory
      forensics libraries and in tools like <code>ReadMemory&lt;T&gt;</code> wrappers you'll find in
      vulnerability research frameworks. Understanding what the template is doing — computing
      <code>sizeof(T)</code> at compile time and casting the raw bytes into the target type — is
      the same understanding required to reason about type confusion in heap exploits.</p>

    <h3>Compilation Errors as a Learning Surface</h3>
    <p>Before attaching to a single game process, the code failed to build. Those failures were
      informative in ways that aren't obvious until you've hit them:</p>
    <div class="breakdown">
      <span class="step">True vs true</span>
      <p>The loop condition was <code>while (True)</code> — correct Python, a hard build error in C++.
        The language is case-sensitive and <code>true</code> is a keyword; <code>True</code> is an
        undeclared identifier. This particular error is a clear sign of switching between languages —
        Python's conventions bleed into C++ unconsciously when you've been writing Python. The
        compiler error is a forcing function that makes the distinction explicit.</p>
      <span class="step">miliseconds typo</span>
      <p><code>std::chrono::miliseconds</code> fails to compile — the correct spelling is
        <code>milliseconds</code>. Python's duck typing means a wrong method name only surfaces at
        runtime; C++ template instantiation catches it at compile time. The difference matters when
        writing code that interacts with system resources.</p>
      <span class="step">missing includes</span>
      <p><code>std::this_thread::sleep_for</code> requires <code>#include &lt;thread&gt;</code> and
        <code>#include &lt;chrono&gt;</code>. Without them, the linker can't resolve the symbol.
        Python's import system obscures this dependency chain; C++ makes every dependency explicit.
        Learning to read "unresolved external symbol" errors teaches you how the linker works,
        which is directly relevant to understanding how DLL injection and function hooking
        operate at the binary level.</p>
      <span class="step">x86 vs x64 mismatch</span>
      <p>The game process (<code>csgo.exe</code>, Classic Offensive) is 32-bit. Building the
        tool as x64 prevents <code>OpenProcess</code> from attaching correctly — the pointer
        widths don't match and the address arithmetic is wrong. Selecting the correct platform
        target in Visual Studio (x86) is a forced encounter with the same bitness
        considerations that matter when writing shellcode or choosing between 32-bit and
        64-bit exploitation paths.</p>
    </div>

    <h3>Windows Defender and the Silent Quarantine</h3>
    <p>Once the build errors were fixed, the compiled binary vanished. The <code>.exe</code>
      was nowhere in the output directory — not a crash, not an error, just absence.
      Windows Defender had quarantined it silently before it could run.</p>
    <p>This is not an edge case. It is the normal outcome when you compile a native binary
      that imports <code>OpenProcess</code>, <code>ReadProcessMemory</code>, and
      <code>WriteProcessMemory</code> — all three appear in the import table, and together
      they constitute exactly the capability set that malware uses to perform process
      injection and credential theft. The AV does not know the binary is a research tool;
      it sees the import table, applies heuristics, and acts accordingly.</p>
    <p>The fix — adding a build output folder exclusion to Defender — is operational security
      knowledge that matters in both directions. A malware analyst reviewing a sample will
      look at the same import table entries to triage intent before running the binary.
      Understanding why the tool was flagged is the same analysis, run in reverse: what
      does the import table tell a static scanner about capability? The answer to that
      question informs both how defenders write signatures and how attackers choose which
      imports to resolve dynamically rather than statically declaring them. That's not a
      lesson you get from reading documentation; it comes from watching your binary
      disappear and having to reason about why.</p>
    <p>The C++ prototype worked as a minimal radar hack after those obstacles were cleared —
      entity list loop, spotted flag write, same primitive as the Python version. But the
      iteration loop was slow. Every offset change required a recompile. Python and
      <code>pymem</code> let offset constants be changed at the top of a script and rerun
      in seconds. For an exploratory research workflow where the same offset might need
      to be re-derived from scratch after a game update, that iteration speed matters
      enormously. The C++ work wasn't wasted — it built the foundational model. The
      switch to Python was a tooling choice, not a retreat.</p>

    <!-- ============================================================ -->
    <h2>Part 1 — The Starting Point: What Is Process Memory?</h2>

    <p>Before writing a line of code, the first problem is conceptual. How does a running Windows
      process store its game state, and how does any external program reach it?</p>
    <p>Windows gives every process its own virtual address space — 4 GB for a 32-bit process like
      CS:CO. The game engine allocates objects on the heap at runtime: players, weapons, world
      geometry. Those objects live at addresses that shift between sessions due to ASLR, but large
      modules like <code>client.dll</code> load at consistent base addresses that can be queried
      through the Windows API. Every value visible on screen — player positions, health, view
      angles — exists in that address space as raw bytes.</p>
    <p><strong>ReadProcessMemory</strong> and <strong>WriteProcessMemory</strong> are the Windows
      API calls that let a process with <code>PROCESS_VM_READ</code> access reach into another
      process's address space. <code>pymem</code> wraps them in Python, handles process attachment
      via <code>OpenProcess</code>, and provides typed read methods (<code>read_float</code>,
      <code>read_int</code>, <code>read_uint</code>) that translate raw bytes into usable values.
      Attaching to the target and querying the module base is the first checkpoint:</p>
    <span class="label">initial attachment</span>
    <pre>pm = pymem.Pymem("csgo.exe")
client = pymem.process.module_from_name(pm.process_handle, "client.dll").lpBaseOfDll</pre>
    <p>If those two lines run without a <code>PermissionError</code>, you're in. The moment you
      print a float from the process's memory and recognise it as a plausible world coordinate,
      something becomes concrete about how exposed a running process's internal state is to
      anything holding the right handle.</p>
    <p>The immediate next thought — and this is the security research framing kicking in — is that
      this is exactly what a debugger does. What a memory forensics tool does against a running
      malware sample. What a vulnerability researcher does when they attach to a target binary.
      The tool is different; the primitive is identical. Starting with a game just makes the
      feedback loop fast: you can see immediately whether your read makes sense.</p>

    <!-- ============================================================ -->
    <h2>Part 2 — Finding the Data: Offset Research</h2>

    <p>The hardest part of this project was not the code. It was offset research — finding the
      fixed distances from a known stable address (<code>client.dll</code>'s base) to the specific
      fields inside game objects. The entity list lives at <code>client.dll + 0x04E2520C</code>.
      An entity's health is at <code>entity_ptr + 0xF8</code>. Position X is at
      <code>entity_ptr + 0x130</code>. None of these are documented anywhere. Finding them is a
      reverse engineering problem.</p>

    <h3>Classic Offensive as an Unusual Target</h3>
    <p>The target is not vanilla CS:GO. Classic Offensive is a community mod that fuses CS:GO's
      engine with the gameplay and asset pipeline of Counter-Strike: Source — two different engine
      generations sharing a single binary. That hybrid makes it a better learning exercise and a
      harder offset problem simultaneously.</p>
    <p>Published CS:GO offset databases (hazedumper, cs2-dumper) exist for the official game
      build. Classic Offensive does not appear in them. Offsets that match vanilla CS:GO
      (<code>m_iTeamNum = 0xF4</code> in some builds) do not match this mod. Every address in the
      constants block had to be sourced independently against this specific binary — which is
      precisely what makes it a better learning exercise. There is no offset dump to copy from.
      You either find it yourself or you don't have it.</p>

    <h3>Cheat Engine as a Dynamic Analysis Tool</h3>
    <p>Cheat Engine is the tool for this. It's a memory scanner and debugger aimed at games, but
      its core functionality — value search, narrowing, pointer tracing — is the same category of
      dynamic analysis as a PIN instrumenter or a Frida hook.</p>
    <p>The methodology: attach to the running game, search for a known value (your current health:
      100). Take damage. Search for the new value (85). The candidate address list drops from
      millions to a handful. Repeat until one address remains. You've located the health field in
      the live heap.</p>
    <p>The problem: raw heap addresses change every session. The goal is an offset from a stable
      base — <code>client.dll</code>, which loads at a reproducible address. Cheat Engine can
      trace pointer chains back from a dynamic address to a module base, exposing the chain of
      pointer dereferences the engine itself uses to reach that field. This is how the
      <code>NAME_BASE_PTR_OFFSET</code> and <code>NAME_CHAIN</code> constants in the code were
      found:</p>
    <span class="label">pointer chain — resolved in code</span>
    <pre>NAME_BASE_PTR_OFFSET = 0x0534D810
NAME_CHAIN           = [0x17C, 0xA0, 0x18, 0x34, 0x310]

entity_list_2 = resolve_pointer_chain(pm, client + NAME_BASE_PTR_OFFSET, NAME_CHAIN)</pre>
    <p>Walking that chain manually in Cheat Engine before translating it to code reveals the
      structure of the engine's internal object graph — which pointer leads to which container,
      at what stride the entity array is indexed. This is identical to the methodology used when
      reverse engineering any binary that manages structured heap data: trace from a known stable
      reference, follow pointers, identify strides and offsets.</p>
    <span class="label">resolve_pointer_chain — step by step</span>
    <pre>def resolve_pointer_chain(pm, base, chain):
    addr = pm.read_uint(base)            # (1) dereference the root pointer
    for offset in chain[:-1]:           # (2) walk every level except the last
        addr = pm.read_uint(addr + offset)
    return addr + chain[-1]             # (3) final offset added, NOT dereferenced</pre>
    <div class="breakdown">
      <span class="step">step 1 — root dereference</span>
      <p><code>pm.read_uint(base)</code> reads 4 bytes at <code>base</code> and interprets them as
        an unsigned integer — an address. This is a pointer dereference: you're not reading a
        value, you're reading where to look next. The result is a heap address somewhere in the
        game's address space.</p>
      <span class="step">step 2 — chain traversal</span>
      <p>For each offset in the chain except the last: add the offset to the current address and
        dereference again. Each iteration lands in a different object — a container, a manager
        class, a sub-struct. The offset is the field position within that object where the next
        pointer lives. This mirrors exactly what the game's own compiled code does when it walks
        its internal data structures.</p>
      <span class="step">step 3 — final offset, no dereference</span>
      <p>The last entry in the chain is added but <em>not</em> dereferenced. It's the byte offset
        of the target field within the final object — the address you pass to
        <code>read_float</code> or <code>write_int</code>. Dereferencing it would read the value,
        which isn't what this function returns; it returns the address so the caller decides how
        to read it.</p>
      <span class="step">why this matters for security research</span>
      <p>Walking a pointer chain in live process memory is structurally identical to traversing an
        object graph in a heap exploit: you follow pointers through allocations to reach a target
        field, and the offsets you use are derived from the binary's internal layout. The same
        reasoning applies in IDA when tracing how a global handle leads through a manager class
        to a vtable entry.</p>
    </div>

    <h3>Finding teamNum — A Case Study in Narrowing</h3>
    <p>The <code>teamNum</code> field (<code>m_iTeamNum</code>) is the simplest value to locate
      and a good introduction to the methodology. It stores which side you're on: 2 for
      Terrorist, 3 for Counter-Terrorist. Its value is predictable and controllable — you can
      switch teams on a local bot server and force the value to change on demand.</p>
    <p>The initial scan produced around ten addresses that all changed between 2 and 3 when teams
      switched. This is expected: the game engine mirrors certain values across multiple data
      structures — the player entity struct, the HUD system, the scoreboard, cached network
      state. Only one of those is the authoritative field inside the player struct that the game's
      own logic reads when it needs to compare teams.</p>
    <p>The disambiguation technique is "Find out what accesses this address" in Cheat Engine.
      For each candidate address, this attaches a breakpoint-style hook and logs every
      instruction that reads or writes that location while the game runs. The pattern to look for
      is a register-plus-offset read — <code>mov eax, [ecx + OFFSET]</code> or
      <code>cmp [esi + OFFSET], 02</code> — because that form indicates the game is accessing a
      field at a consistent byte position within a struct, which is what an entity field looks
      like at the assembly level.</p>
    <p>For this mod two addresses showed <code>[ecx + 0xB8]</code> and <code>[ecx + 0x158]</code>
      as write destinations. Both were valid — the engine writes the team number to both
      locations. The read that settled it was:</p>
    <span class="label">the decisive instruction — cheat engine capture</span>
    <pre>cmp dword ptr [esi+00000158], 02</pre>
    <p>This is the game's own team comparison logic — checking whether an entity's team value
      equals 2. It's reading from <code>+0x158</code>. That's the field the game's decision logic
      consults; that's the one to use. The <code>+0xB8</code> field exists (it's written to) but
      isn't what the game reads for team decisions — likely a legacy field from the Source Engine
      ancestor code that Classic Offensive inherits. Using the wrong one would mean your team
      filter reads a value the game logic never actually checks.</p>

    <h3>Finding localPlayer — The Pointer Scan Grind</h3>
    <p>The <code>localPlayer</code> offset is more involved. It's not a static value to scan for;
      it's a static pointer — an address inside <code>client.dll</code> that always holds the
      current address of the local player's entity struct. The entity struct address changes
      every session (heap allocation); the pointer to it does not.</p>
    <p>The process: find the team number address for <em>your</em> entity specifically, trace back
      through "Find out what accesses this address" to get the base register value (<code>ecx</code>
      or <code>esi</code>), read that register — that's the live entity struct address. Then run
      a pointer scan in Cheat Engine: search all of memory for a location that holds that address.
      The result that comes from inside <code>client.dll</code>'s static data segment is the
      localPlayer pointer.</p>
    <p>In practice this produced thousands of candidates on the first pass. Narrowing them
      requires the rescan technique: restart the match so the entity struct gets reallocated to a
      new address, find the new entity struct address using the same register-read method, then
      rescan the pointer scan results to keep only entries that now point to the <em>new</em>
      address. Pointers rooted in <code>client.dll</code>'s static memory will follow the new
      allocation; random heap pointers that happened to hold the old address won't. Repeat the
      cycle two or three times and the list drops from thousands to a handful, then to one stable
      <code>client.dll + OFFSET</code> entry.</p>
    <p>What made this harder: Cheat Engine's debugger caused an access violation on the first
      breakpoint attempt. The fix — switching the debugger method in CE settings from the default
      to VEH (Vectored Exception Handler) — is a practical encounter with how Windows exception
      dispatch works. VEH fires before a structured exception handler, making it less visible to
      software that monitors for standard debugger breakpoints. Anti-cheat systems use this
      distinction; knowing it from the attacker side is the prerequisite for understanding why
      kernel-mode anti-cheat moved to KPP-protected ring-0 monitors rather than userspace
      exception hooks.</p>
    <p>That cycle — locate, verify, break, re-locate — is exactly the cycle a malware analyst
      faces when a sample updates its internal structure to evade a signature. The patience
      required is the same. You can't shortcut it.</p>

    <h3>The Patience Problem</h3>
    <p>Offsets break on game updates. A patch shifts the entity list layout and suddenly health
      reads return garbage — usually <code>-1</code> or a suspiciously round number that doesn't
      match anything real. The only response is going back to Cheat Engine, re-scanning, re-tracing
      the pointer chain, and updating the constants. Across six versions of the tool this happened
      multiple times.</p>
    <p>That cycle — locate, verify, break, re-locate — is exactly the cycle a malware analyst
      faces when a sample updates its internal structure to evade a signature. The patience
      required is the same. You can't shortcut it.</p>

    <!-- ============================================================ -->
    <h2>Part 3 — The Overlay: A Transparent Fullscreen Window</h2>

    <p>Drawing on top of the game requires a surface that is: fullscreen, always on top, visually
      transparent, and non-interactive (mouse clicks must fall through to the game). Tkinter
      provides the drawing surface; two Win32 extended window style flags handle the rest.</p>
    <span class="label">overlay setup — transparent, click-through</span>
    <pre>root.attributes("-topmost", True)
root.attributes("-transparentcolor", "black")
root.overrideredirect(True)   # removes title bar and window chrome
root.geometry(f"{sw}x{sh}+0+0")

canvas = tk.Canvas(root, width=sw, height=sh, bg="black", highlightthickness=0)

hwnd = ctypes.windll.user32.FindWindowW(None, root.title())
make_window_clickthrough(hwnd)</pre>
    <span class="label">win32 click-through via ctypes</span>
    <pre>GWL_EXSTYLE       = -20
WS_EX_LAYERED     = 0x80000
WS_EX_TRANSPARENT = 0x20
LWA_COLORKEY      = 0x1

styles = ctypes.windll.user32.GetWindowLongW(hwnd, GWL_EXSTYLE)
styles |= WS_EX_LAYERED | WS_EX_TRANSPARENT
ctypes.windll.user32.SetWindowLongW(hwnd, GWL_EXSTYLE, styles)
ctypes.windll.user32.SetLayeredWindowAttributes(hwnd, 0x000000, 0, LWA_COLORKEY)</pre>
    <p><code>WS_EX_LAYERED</code> enables color-key transparency — anything drawn in pure black
      becomes invisible. <code>WS_EX_TRANSPARENT</code> makes the window non-interactive: the OS
      routes mouse and keyboard events to whichever window is below it in the Z-order, which is
      the game. The canvas clears and redraws every ~30ms.</p>
    <p>The first problem here was timing. Tkinter's HWND doesn't exist until after the first
      layout pass completes. Calling <code>FindWindowW</code> immediately after
      <code>root.geometry()</code> returns NULL — the window isn't registered yet.
      <code>root.update_idletasks()</code> forces the layout pass before the Win32 call. Without
      it, <code>FindWindowW</code> returns NULL silently, the click-through setup never runs, and
      the overlay blocks all mouse input to the game. A silent failure that took a while to
      diagnose because nothing visibly breaks until you try to click something in-game.</p>

    <!-- ============================================================ -->
    <h2>Part 4 — ESP: Projecting the World onto the Screen</h2>

    <p>ESP (Extra Sensory Perception) draws boxes, health bars, and labels around entities. The
      central problem: entity positions exist in 3D world space; the overlay is 2D screen space.
      The game's view matrix bridges them.</p>

    <h3>World-to-Screen Projection</h3>
    <p>The view matrix is a 4×4 float matrix at <code>client.dll + 0x4DF85A4</code>. It encodes
      the camera's orientation and projection transform. Multiplying a 3D world coordinate through
      it produces a clip-space coordinate that normalises to a pixel position:</p>
    <span class="label">world_to_screen — clip-space projection</span>
    <pre>def world_to_screen(pos, matrix, sw, sh):
    x, y, z = pos
    clip_x = x*matrix[0]  + y*matrix[1]  + z*matrix[2]  + matrix[3]
    clip_y = x*matrix[4]  + y*matrix[5]  + z*matrix[6]  + matrix[7]
    clip_w = x*matrix[12] + y*matrix[13] + z*matrix[14] + matrix[15]
    if clip_w &lt; 0.01:
        return None          # entity is behind the camera
    ndc_x = clip_x / clip_w
    ndc_y = clip_y / clip_w
    return int(sw/2 + ndc_x*sw/2), int(sh/2 - ndc_y*sh/2)</pre>
    <div class="breakdown">
      <span class="step">step 1 — matrix multiply into clip space</span>
      <p>The view matrix is 16 floats laid out row-major. Multiplying the world position
        <code>(x, y, z, 1)</code> through it produces three clip-space components.
        <code>clip_x</code> and <code>clip_y</code> use rows 0 and 1 of the matrix.
        <code>clip_w</code> uses row 3 (indices 12–15) — the perspective row that encodes depth.
        The implicit fourth input component is 1, which is why the <code>matrix[3]</code>,
        <code>matrix[7]</code>, and <code>matrix[15]</code> translation terms are added directly.</p>
      <span class="step">step 2 — the w component and perspective divide</span>
      <p><code>clip_w</code> encodes how far the point is from the camera along the view axis.
        Dividing <code>clip_x</code> and <code>clip_y</code> by it is the perspective divide —
        it shrinks coordinates for distant points (large w) and expands them for close ones
        (small w), producing the natural foreshortening effect. After the divide,
        <code>ndc_x</code> and <code>ndc_y</code> live in the range [−1, 1]: NDC
        (Normalized Device Coordinates).</p>
      <span class="step">step 3 — ndc to pixel</span>
      <p><code>ndc_x = −1</code> is the left screen edge, <code>+1</code> is the right.
        Mapping to pixels: <code>sw/2 + ndc_x * sw/2</code>. For Y the sign flips —
        NDC Y increases upward but screen Y increases downward, so the formula is
        <code>sh/2 − ndc_y * sh/2</code>. A point dead centre of the camera (ndc 0, 0) maps
        to the screen centre.</p>
      <span class="step">step 4 — the clip_w guard</span>
      <p>If <code>clip_w</code> is negative the point is behind the camera's near clip plane.
        Dividing by a negative number inverts the sign of both NDC values, mirroring the
        projected position to the opposite side of the screen. Early versions drew boxes in the
        wrong screen quadrant for entities directly behind the player — the guard short-circuits
        that case entirely by returning <code>None</code>.</p>
    </div>

    <h3>Entity Loop and Box Drawing</h3>
    <p>The entity list holds up to 64 entries. For each slot: read the entity pointer, skip empty
      or invalid pointers, skip dead entities (health ≤ 0) and local player, project foot and head
      positions, derive the box from the head-to-foot pixel distance:</p>
    <span class="label">entity loop — box construction</span>
    <pre>feet_screen = world_to_screen((ex, ey, ez),     view_matrix, sw, sh)
head_screen = world_to_screen((ex, ey, head_z), view_matrix, sw, sh)

box_h = fy - hy            # pixel height foot-to-head
box_w = box_h // 2         # proportional width
x1, y1 = fx - box_w//2, hy
x2, y2 = fx + box_w//2, fy

canvas.create_rectangle(x1, y1, x2, y2, outline=box_color, width=2)</pre>
    <p>The proportional width (half the height) keeps boxes plausible at all distances — a player
      far away gets a thin narrow box, close up a wider one. The head Z calculation factors in
      crouch state via a delta interpolated from a crouch float offset:</p>
    <pre>delta  = max(0.0, min(1.0, (72.0 - crouch) / 18.0))
head_z = ez + 70 - (HEAD_OFFSET * delta)</pre>
    <p>Without the crouch factor, crouched players show a box that overshoots the head — the foot
      position is correct but the head calculation assumes standing height. The delta smoothly
      interpolates between standing and crouched head height as the crouch float transitions.</p>

    <h3>Bone Matrix and Head Circle</h3>
    <p>The flat box felt imprecise for the aimbot's benefit. The game stores a bone matrix for
      each entity at <code>entity_ptr + 0x26A0</code> — an array of 4×3 float matrices, one per
      bone. Reading the highest-Z bone among IDs 6, 8, and 12 (neck/head area) gives an exact
      world-space head position:</p>
    <span class="label">bone matrix — head position extraction</span>
    <pre>bm_ptr = pm.read_uint(ent_ptr + 0x26A0)
best_bone  = None
highest_bz = -float("inf")
for bone_id in [6, 8, 12]:
    off  = bone_id * 0x30
    bx_  = pm.read_float(bm_ptr + off + 0x0C)
    by_  = pm.read_float(bm_ptr + off + 0x1C)
    bz_  = pm.read_float(bm_ptr + off + 0x2C)
    if bz_ &gt; highest_bz:
        highest_bz = bz_
        best_bone  = (bx_, by_, bz_)</pre>
    <p>Projecting the head bone and a virtual point 7 units above it gives a pixel radius that
      scales naturally with distance. The circle is drawn on top of the box and served as the
      aimbot's visual confirmation that the bone target was being resolved correctly before angle
      writes were added.</p>

    <h3>Flicker and the Tkinter Thread Problem</h3>
    <p>Early versions flickered badly. The cause: calling <code>canvas.create_rectangle()</code>
      directly from a background thread. Tkinter's internal state is not thread-safe — concurrent
      access from the ESP thread and the main Tkinter event loop produced intermittent corruption
      and visual tearing. The fix was keeping all canvas operations inside the ESP thread but
      ensuring it ran on the main thread's after() scheduler. Alternatively, running the ESP loop
      itself as the main thread content (which is what the final architecture does) and using
      <code>time.sleep(0.03)</code> as the frame gate.</p>

    <!-- ============================================================ -->
    <h2>Part 5 — Aimbot: Writing Angles</h2>

    <p>Reading game state is one kind of access. Writing to it is another. The aimbot reads the
      current view angles, computes where they need to point, and writes the corrected values
      back into the game's memory — overriding what the mouse produced. This is
      <code>WriteProcessMemory</code> used offensively.</p>

    <h3>Angle Math</h3>
    <p>The view angles live behind a two-level pointer rooted in
      <code>stdshader_dx9.dll</code> — a non-obvious location that took CE pointer scanning to
      find. Pitch (vertical) and yaw (horizontal) are stored as floats. Computing the required
      angle to a target is straightforward trigonometry:</p>
    <span class="label">angle calculation — atan2 pitch/yaw</span>
    <pre>def calc_angle(src, dst):
    dx, dy, dz = dst[0]-src[0], dst[1]-src[1], dst[2]-src[2]
    hyp = math.sqrt(dx*dx + dy*dy)
    return -math.degrees(math.atan2(dz, hyp)), math.degrees(math.atan2(dy, dx))</pre>
    <p>Pitch is negated because CS:CO's coordinate system has positive pitch pointing downward
      (looking up requires a negative value). Yaw wrapping is the subtle problem: angles live in
      (-180, 180] and when a target crosses the ±180 boundary, naive subtraction gives a ~360°
      jump instead of a small step. The <code>angle_diff</code> function normalises it:</p>
    <pre>def angle_diff(a1, a2):
    dp = a1[0] - a2[0]
    dy = ((a1[1]-a2[1])+180) % 360 - 180
    return math.sqrt(dp*dp + dy*dy)</pre>
    <div class="breakdown">
      <span class="step">calc_angle — what atan2 is doing</span>
      <p><code>dx, dy, dz</code> is the raw displacement vector from the local player's feet to
        the target bone. <code>hyp</code> is the horizontal ground distance — the length of the
        vector projected onto the XY plane, ignoring height. <code>atan2(dz, hyp)</code> gives
        the vertical angle above or below the horizontal — if the target is above you, dz is
        positive and atan2 returns a positive angle. CS:CO's pitch convention is inverted (positive
        pitch = looking down), so the result is negated. <code>atan2(dy, dx)</code> gives the
        compass bearing on the XY plane — yaw, ranging from −180° to +180°.</p>
      <span class="step">angle_diff — the yaw wrap problem</span>
      <p>Imagine the local player is facing 179° and the target is at −179°. The real angular gap
        is 2°. But naive subtraction gives <code>179 − (−179) = 358°</code> — the long way around
        the circle. This makes the target appear to be 358° outside the FOV cone, so it's never
        selected as a valid aim target. The formula <code>((a1−a2) + 180) % 360 − 180</code> fixes
        it: adding 180 shifts the result into [0°, 360°), modulo 360 wraps it, subtracting 180
        maps it back to [−180°, +180°). The result is always the shortest angular distance between
        two headings regardless of which side of the ±180° boundary they sit on.</p>
    </div>

    <h3>Bezier Smoothing</h3>
    <p>Snapping directly to the target angle is instantly detectable — a zero-latency perfect
      correction is a statistical impossibility in real mouse input. The aim interpolates along a
      cubic Bezier curve instead, with a parameter <code>t</code> that increments each frame:</p>
    <span class="label">bezier smooth angle — cubic interpolation</span>
    <pre>def bezier_smooth_angle(current, target, t):
    p0, y0 = current
    p3, y3 = target
    p1 = p0 + (p3-p0)*0.25    # control point 1 — 25% along
    p2 = p0 + (p3-p0)*0.75    # control point 2 — 75% along
    y1 = y0 + ((y3-y0+180)%360-180)*0.25
    y2 = y0 + ((y3-y0+180)%360-180)*0.75
    return bezier_point(p0,p1,p2,p3,t), bezier_point(y0,y1,y2,y3,t)</pre>
    <div class="breakdown">
      <span class="step">step 1 — the cubic bernstein formula</span>
      <p><code>bezier_point</code> evaluates the standard cubic Bézier polynomial:
        <code>B(t) = (1−t)³·P0 + 3(1−t)²t·P1 + 3(1−t)t²·P2 + t³·P3</code>.
        <code>u = 1 − t</code> is just the complement of <code>t</code> — shorthand to avoid
        repeating <code>(1−t)</code> four times. P0 is the start, P3 is the end, and P1/P2 are
        control points that shape the curve between them.</p>
      <span class="step">step 2 — what t does at the extremes</span>
      <p>At <code>t = 0</code>: <code>u = 1</code>, so <code>u³·P0 = P0</code> and every other
        term has a <code>t</code> factor that collapses to zero — result is exactly P0, the
        current angle. At <code>t = 1</code>: <code>u = 0</code>, so every term with a
        <code>u</code> factor collapses — result is exactly P3, the target angle. Between 0 and 1
        the four terms blend smoothly, with their weights always summing to 1.</p>
      <span class="step">step 3 — control points at 25% and 75%</span>
      <p><code>p1 = p0 + (p3−p0)·0.25</code> places the first control point a quarter of the
        way along the direct line from start to target. <code>p2</code> sits at 75%. With both
        control points on the straight line between start and end, the curve doesn't deviate
        sideways — it stays on the direct angular path. But the cubic weighting still produces
        ease-in/ease-out: the aim accelerates leaving the start and decelerates arriving at the
        target, because the high-order <code>t³</code> and <code>u³</code> terms dominate near
        the endpoints and change slowly there.</p>
      <span class="step">step 4 — yaw wrapping inside bezier_smooth_angle</span>
      <p>Yaw can't be interpolated naively. If current yaw is 170° and the target is −170°, a
        straight lerp sweeps 340° the long way around. The control point offsets use the same
        shortest-path trick as <code>angle_diff</code>:
        <code>((y3−y0+180)%360−180)</code> gives the signed shortest delta, and the control
        points are placed along that arc rather than across the ±180° boundary.</p>
      <span class="step">step 5 — why this fools timing analysis</span>
      <p>A raw memory write that snaps the angle to the target in one frame produces a velocity
        spike — an instantaneous delta of potentially 90°+ in 10ms, which is physically
        impossible for a human wrist. Anti-cheat systems that log view angle deltas per frame
        will flag this as a statistical outlier. The Bezier spreads the same total angular
        movement across many frames with a natural deceleration profile, making the per-frame
        deltas indistinguishable from fast human mouse input.</p>
    </div>
    <p>The smoothing slider in the config GUI controls how fast <code>t</code> increments per
      frame — which is the only dial between instant and slow. At the high end, <code>t</code>
      reaches 1 in a single step: the aim locks onto the head in one frame. At the low end it
      creeps over many frames, producing a gradual arc that reads as human tracking. The Bezier
      curve runs in all three cases — even at maximum speed the movement still follows the cubic
      interpolation path rather than a raw write, keeping the per-frame angle delta within a
      range that is physically plausible for fast human input.</p>

    <h3>FOV Cone — Aimbot Activation Radius</h3>
    <p>The aimbot doesn't lock onto every enemy on the map. It only considers targets whose
      required aim angle is within a configurable angular distance from the current crosshair —
      the FOV cone. This is controlled by <code>desired_fov</code>, a degree value set via slider
      (range 1°–30°):</p>
    <span class="label">fov gate — aimbot_thread</span>
    <pre>best_fov = shared["desired_fov"]   # start with the threshold as the minimum
...
fov = angle_diff(angle, (cp, cy))  # angular distance: crosshair to enemy
if fov &lt; best_fov:
    best_fov   = fov               # narrowest match wins
    best_angle = angle
    new_target = ent_ptr</pre>
    <p><code>angle_diff</code> returns the shortest angular distance between the current view
      direction and the direction to the enemy head. If that distance is larger than
      <code>desired_fov</code>, the enemy is outside the cone and the aimbot ignores them
      entirely — the crosshair must already be pointing roughly at the target for the lock to
      activate. Among all enemies inside the cone, the one with the smallest angular offset wins.</p>
    <p>On the overlay, this cone can be visualised as a circle drawn at the screen centre — a
      small crosshair-centred ring whose pixel radius corresponds to the degree threshold. A
      tight FOV (1°–3°) draws a small circle and requires the player to manually aim near the
      head before the aimbot engages; a wide FOV (15°–30°) draws a large circle and activates
      across much of the screen. The circle is the same concept as a sniper scope — it defines
      the zone of engagement rather than doing the aiming itself. Keeping it small is also a
      deliberate anti-detection measure: a wide FOV that locks onto enemies anywhere on screen
      is a statistical signature, while a small FOV is indistinguishable from a precise human
      flick.</p>

    <h3>Recoil Correction</h3>
    <p>Writing the correct angle to a stationary target is one problem. Keeping it on a moving
      crosshair while the gun is firing is another. Automatic weapons produce upward spray —
      the game's recoil system shifts the view angle upward with each shot, and the aimbot write
      from the previous frame is immediately overridden by the recoil offset.</p>
    <p>The correction is a running pitch offset applied while the left mouse button is held,
      accumulating over time toward a cap of 54 degrees:</p>
    <span class="label">recoil compensation — live pitch offset</span>
    <pre>if shared["firing"]:
    if shared["left_hold_start"] is None:
        shared["left_hold_start"] = time.time()
    if time.time() - shared["left_hold_start"] &gt;= 0.05:
        shared["current_pitch_correction"] = min(
            shared["current_pitch_correction"] + shared["correction_speed_2"] * shared["recoil"],
            54.0
        )</pre>
    <p>Getting <code>correction_speed_2</code> right required iterative live testing — too fast
      and the aim overcorrects and dips below the target, too slow and it can't keep up with
      the spray. F4/F5/F6 hotkeys set recoil presets (40 / 55 / 20) because the right value
      varies by weapon fire rate and spread pattern.</p>

    <!-- ============================================================ -->
    <h2>Part 6 — Triggerbot: Monitoring a Pointer Chain</h2>

    <p>The triggerbot doesn't detect crosshair contact geometrically. It monitors a specific
      memory value via a pointer chain and fires the mouse when that value changes. The value
      encodes something internal to the game's hit detection — when the crosshair crosses an
      enemy hitbox, a game-internal counter updates.</p>
    <span class="label">triggerbot — pointer chain and fire logic</span>
    <pre>TB_POINTER_BASE = 0x00DEF97C
TB_OFFSETS      = [0x24, 0x4, 0x68]

ptr   = tb_resolve_pointer(pm, client + TB_POINTER_BASE, TB_OFFSETS)
value = tb_read_value(pm, ptr, "FLOAT")

elif value != prev_value:
    m.press(mouse.Button.left)
    time.sleep(0.05)
    m.release(mouse.Button.left)</pre>
    <p>This approach is more robust than geometric detection: it uses the game's own hit
      detection logic rather than external math that might disagree with the server. The tradeoff
      is brittleness — the pointer chain breaks on game updates and requires re-sourcing from
      Cheat Engine each time.</p>
    <p>The 20ms pre-press delay and 50ms hold are humanisation constants. A zero-latency instant
      click is a statistical anomaly in real input data. Some anti-cheat systems maintain timing
      histograms of click events and flag inputs that fall outside the human-achievable minimum
      latency. Understanding that detection surface is part of what this project teaches — not
      just how to fire, but what the defender is measuring.</p>

    <!-- ============================================================ -->
    <h2>Part 7 — Radar: Writing to Game State</h2>

    <p>The radar hack is the simplest feature but the most direct demonstration of
      WriteProcessMemory used offensively. Rather than reading a value and rendering it externally,
      it modifies the entity's in-game flags directly so the game's own minimap renders
      enemies as visible:</p>
    <span class="label">radar — forcing entity flags via write</span>
    <pre>RADAR_FORCE  = {0x0934: 256, 0x0978: 1}
RADAR_TOGGLE = {0x0A20: 1,   0x0A24: 1}

for offset, desired in RADAR_FORCE.items():
    if pm.read_int(ent_ptr + offset) != desired:
        pm.write_int(ent_ptr + offset, desired)</pre>
    <p>The read-before-write pattern avoids unnecessary writes — only writing when the value
      has drifted keeps the write volume low, which matters because VAC-style anti-cheats can
      monitor write frequency to protected memory regions. The flag toggling (RADAR_TOGGLE)
      alternates each loop iteration to keep a watchdog from detecting a static value that
      never naturally changes.</p>
    <p>This is the same primitive used in process injection: writing controlled bytes into
      another process's memory to alter its behaviour. The target here is a game flag rather
      than a shellcode stub, but the Windows API call is identical.</p>
    <p>The radar was also the first feature that worked in the C++ prototype — the same entity
      loop, the same spotted flag write, just expressed in <code>memory.Write&lt;bool&gt;(entity +
      offsets::spotted, true)</code> instead of pymem. Seeing the enemy dot appear on the
      minimap in response to a write from an external process is the moment the abstraction
      collapses: the game doesn't know who wrote that byte.</p>

    <!-- ============================================================ -->
    <h2>Part 8 — Threading Architecture</h2>

    <p>Running everything in a single loop would mean the ESP drawing blocks the aimbot, which
      blocks the triggerbot. Each feature needs its own update rate and must not starve the
      others. The solution is a thread-per-feature architecture using a shared dict as the
      message bus:</p>
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Thread</th><th>Interval</th><th>Role</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>detect_local_player</td><td>10 s</td><td>Identifies local entity index and team</td></tr>
        <tr><td>update_entity_info</td><td>100 ms</td><td>Fills entity position cache</td></tr>
        <tr><td>esp_loop</td><td>30 ms</td><td>Owns all canvas drawing (≈33 fps)</td></tr>
        <tr><td>aimbot_thread</td><td>10 ms</td><td>Reads angles, writes corrected values</td></tr>
        <tr><td>radar_thread</td><td>50 ms</td><td>Writes entity visibility flags</td></tr>
        <tr><td>triggerbot_thread</td><td>10 ms</td><td>Monitors pointer chain, fires on change</td></tr>
        <tr><td>combined_hotkey_listener</td><td>event</td><td>pynput callbacks for all hotkeys</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>Race conditions exist: multiple threads read and write <code>shared["my_team"]</code>,
      <code>shared["aim_active"]</code>, and similar flags without locks. Python's GIL makes
      individual dict reads and writes atomically safe for simple scalar values, so a stale read
      here produces at worst a miscoloured box for one frame or a missed trigger event — not a
      crash. In production code this warrants <code>threading.Lock()</code> around the critical
      sections. Here it was a conscious tradeoff: adding locks around every shared access would
      have been noise given the cosmetic-only failure mode.</p>

    <h3>detect_local_player — Fingerprinting Your Own Entity</h3>
    <p>The entity list contains every player in the server, including the local player. But the
      game doesn't store "your entity is at index N" at a fixed, stable offset — the index
      depends on server connection order and shifts between matches. Every other feature needs
      to know which entity is you, both to skip drawing a box over yourself and to read your
      own position as the aimbot's source. The solution is a fingerprinting loop:</p>
    <span class="label">detect_local_player — matching by position and health</span>
    <pre>base_ptr     = pm.read_uint(client + 0x0526108C)
local_health = pm.read_int(base_ptr + 0x100)
local_x      = pm.read_float(base_ptr + 0x138)
local_z      = pm.read_float(base_ptr + 0x140)

for i in range(MAX_ENTITIES):
    d = math.sqrt((ex - local_x)**2 + (ez - local_z)**2)
    if d &lt; 2.0 and abs(eh - local_health) &lt; 1 and d &lt; closest_dist:
        closest_idx = i</pre>
    <div class="breakdown">
      <span class="step">step 1 — read local state from a known pointer</span>
      <p>The local player's raw data (position, health) is accessible via a separate stable
        pointer at <code>client + 0x0526108C</code> — a different path into the engine's local
        player object that doesn't go through the entity list at all. This gives ground truth
        values to match against.</p>
      <span class="step">step 2 — XZ distance matching</span>
      <p>For every entity slot, compute the 2D ground-plane distance between the entity's XZ
        position and the known local position. Y is omitted because vertical position can differ
        slightly depending on how the engine interpolates the local player versus the networked
        entity representation. The 2.0 world-unit threshold accounts for this — it's tight enough
        to exclude any other player but loose enough for the representation difference.</p>
      <span class="step">step 3 — health cross-check</span>
      <p>Position alone isn't enough — two entities could briefly share the same position during
        a spawn. The health cross-check <code>abs(eh − local_health) &lt; 1</code> adds a second
        independent dimension. Both conditions must be true simultaneously, making a false positive
        essentially impossible in practice.</p>
      <span class="step">step 4 — 10-second refresh rate</span>
      <p>The thread sleeps 10 seconds between scans rather than running every frame. The local
        entity index is stable within a round — it only changes when the player disconnects and
        reconnects. Scanning every frame would burn 64 memory reads every 10ms for a value that
        virtually never changes. The slow refresh is a deliberate read-volume optimisation, the
        same reasoning behind the radar's read-before-write pattern.</p>
    </div>

    <!-- ============================================================ -->
    <h2>Part 9 — The Build Order: How It Actually Came Together</h2>

    <p>The order things were built in was not the order they appear in this writeup. The writeup is
      organised by subsystem for clarity; the real development followed the path of least
      resistance — start with the simplest thing that proves the memory access works, then climb
      toward the features that depend on it. Each stage produced the offsets and the understanding
      the next stage needed.</p>

    <h3>Stage −1 — The C++ Prototype (before Python)</h3>
    <p>Before any Python was written, there was a C++ Visual Studio project. The goal was the same
      — radar hack first, prove the memory access works — but the implementation was native Win32:
      a <code>Memory</code> class wrapping <code>OpenProcess</code>,
      <code>ReadProcessMemory</code>, and <code>WriteProcessMemory</code> directly, with a
      templated <code>Read&lt;T&gt;</code> and <code>Write&lt;T&gt;</code> interface that made
      the type explicit at the call site.</p>
    <p>That prototype hit every obstacle described in the Prologue: compilation errors from Python
      habits (<code>True</code>, <code>miliseconds</code>), missing includes, platform target
      mismatch, and Windows Defender quarantining the binary silently before it could run. Each
      obstacle was a forced encounter with something real — how the C++ toolchain works, how AV
      heuristics classify binaries, how 32-bit/64-bit process boundaries work.</p>
    <p>The switch to Python was made for iteration speed. Every offset tweak in C++ required a
      full recompile. In Python, the same change is a one-line edit at the top of a script and a
      re-run. For a research workflow where the same offset might break after a game update and
      need to be re-derived from scratch with Cheat Engine, that cycle time matters enormously.
      The C++ prototype was not abandoned because C++ was wrong — it was superseded because
      Python was faster for this particular research loop.</p>

    <h3>Stage 0 — Research, before any code</h3>
    <p>The first step was not Cheat Engine, it was reading. Working out conceptually how an ESP and
      an aimbot are even possible: what a view matrix is and why a world-to-screen projection needs
      one, what an "entity list" means in a Source-engine game, how view angles are stored and
      written back. Not copying code — understanding the primitives well enough to know what to go
      looking for in memory. Going in blind with a scanner and no model of the data would have been
      hopeless; knowing in advance that a 4×4 float matrix and a flat array of entity pointers
      <em>had</em> to exist somewhere is what made the search tractable.</p>

    <h3>Stage 1 — Radar (the way in)</h3>
    <p>The radar was built first because it needs no rendering, no math, no projection — just the
      core memory-scanning loop in its purest form. The method was a direct application of the
      known-value-change technique: look directly at an enemy so they show up (spotted) on the
      radar, freeze the scan, then look away so they drop off it, and scan again. The flag that
      flips between those two states is the "spotted" byte. Forcing it to stay set is the entire
      radar hack.</p>
    <p>This stage was the proving ground for everything after it. It confirmed the process
      attachment worked, that <code>client.dll</code>'s base was stable, and that the
      change / narrow / repeat loop actually converged on a real field. A win small enough to reach
      quickly, but real enough to validate the whole approach before sinking time into rendering.
      The same feature had already worked in the C++ prototype — seeing it work again in Python
      with the same underlying primitives confirmed the approach, not just the code.</p>

    <h3>Stage 2 — ESP (where the real structure work happened)</h3>
    <p>ESP is where the bulk of the reverse engineering lived, because it meant mapping the engine's
      object graph rather than flipping a single flag. It came together in a strict dependency
      order, each step unlocking the next:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>The entity list</strong> — finding the base array that holds a pointer to every
        player in the server. Everything downstream indexes off this.</li>
      <li><strong>The local player</strong> — working out which entry in that list is "me", so the
        tool can skip drawing over itself and, later, read its own position as the aimbot's origin.
        This was the hardest single offset to find — the pointer scan grind described in Part 2,
        with thousands of candidates requiring multiple rescan iterations to reduce to one.</li>
      <li><strong>Enemy positions</strong> — reading the world-space coordinates off each entity and
        running them through the world-to-screen projection to land a box on screen.</li>
      <li><strong>Bone matrices — the big one.</strong> Finding the per-entity bone matrix pointer
        and the per-bone stride was the single hardest and most valuable piece of the whole
        project.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>The bone matrix mattered <em>twofold</em>, and that is exactly why it was worth the effort.
      First, for ESP itself: the highest head bone gives an exact point to draw a head circle on,
      and the full bone set lets the overlay draw a complete skeleton instead of a flat box. Second
      — and this is what made it pay off later — it gave the aimbot a <em>real</em> head position to
      target. Without it, an aimbot can only estimate the head as a fixed offset above the player's
      feet, which drifts the instant the player crouches, leans, or plays an animation. With the
      bone matrix, the aim locks onto the actual rendered head bone. The ESP skeleton and the
      aimbot's accuracy came out of the same discovery — one piece of reverse engineering, two
      payoffs.</p>

    <h3>Stage 3 — Aimbot (step-by-step enumeration)</h3>
    <p>With positions and the bone-derived head already solved, the aimbot was a matter of
      methodically enumerating the remaining pieces: locating the view-angle pointers (pitch and
      yaw, reached through a chain rooted in <code>stdshader_dx9.dll</code>), working out the angle
      math to point from the local player to a target bone, then layering on the FOV gate, the
      Bezier smoothing, and the recoil offset one at a time. Each was added and tested in isolation
      before the next went in — stacking unverified changes on a system that writes to live game
      memory is how you end up unable to tell which change broke it.</p>

    <h3>Stage 4 — Triggerbot (rudimentary, still in progress)</h3>
    <p>The triggerbot was the last thing added and is deliberately the least finished. It is a quick,
      rudimentary implementation: monitor a single pointer-chain value that changes on crosshair
      contact, and click when it changes. It works, but it is the part that most clearly still needs
      work — the fire-delay humanisation is a recent addition, the pointer chain is brittle across
      game updates, and the whole feature wants the same step-by-step hardening the aimbot got
      rather than the rushed first pass it currently has.</p>

    <p>The through-line across all four stages: build the simplest thing that proves the access
      works, then let each stage hand its offsets and its understanding up to the next. The radar
      proved the scan loop; the ESP mapped the object graph and found the bone matrix; the bone
      matrix made the aimbot accurate; the triggerbot is the loose end the same methodology hasn't
      been fully applied to yet.</p>


     <p>POC:</p>
    <div class="video">
      <iframe src="https://www.youtube.com/embed/s0z4qB3la-s" title="POC" allowfullscreen></iframe>
    </div>
    <p class="vid-cap">fig. 1 — Proof of Concept</p>

    <!-- ============================================================ -->
    <h2>How This Maps to Security Research</h2>

    <p>Every component of this project has a direct parallel in real security work. The mapping
      is not metaphorical — it's the same API calls, the same methodology, a different target.
      This is why building it matters: you cannot fake the understanding that comes from having
      actually done it. Reading about ReadProcessMemory is not the same as watching Windows
      Defender quarantine your binary because of what ReadProcessMemory implies about your
      intent.</p>

    <h3>ReadProcessMemory as a Research Primitive</h3>
    <p>This is exactly what a debugger does when it reads a target process's memory. What a
      memory forensics tool does when analysing a live malware sample with Volatility or
      Rekall. What a vulnerability researcher does when they attach to a process under x64dbg
      and inspect the heap after a controlled allocation. The Windows primitive is
      <code>ReadProcessMemory</code> in all cases. Building a tool that uses it directly,
      without the debugger abstraction layer, makes the underlying mechanism concrete in a way
      that reading documentation does not.</p>

    <h3>AV Heuristics and the Import Table</h3>
    <p>The C++ prototype being quarantined by Windows Defender before it ever ran is a security
      lesson in its own right. The binary did nothing — it hadn't even executed. The AV acted on
      its static characteristics: <code>OpenProcess</code>, <code>ReadProcessMemory</code>, and
      <code>WriteProcessMemory</code> in the import table together constitute a capability
      fingerprint that matches process injection, credential theft, and game cheating tooling.
      Static analysis flagged it before dynamic analysis had any data.</p>
    <p>A malware analyst triaging an unknown binary runs the same analysis in the same order:
      import table first (what can this binary do?), then strings, then dynamic behaviour.
      Understanding why the tool was flagged is understanding how that first-pass triage works.
      It also surfaces the evasion techniques that attackers use — resolving imports dynamically
      at runtime via <code>GetProcAddress</code> rather than declaring them statically removes
      them from the import table and defeats that heuristic. Knowing the heuristic forces you to
      understand the bypass, and vice versa.</p>

    <h3>Pointer Chain Traversal</h3>
    <p>Walking a multi-level pointer chain to locate a target object in a live heap is the same
      skill used in use-after-free exploitation: understanding object lifetimes, pointer graphs,
      and what memory looks like after a free. It's the same skill used when reverse engineering
      a compiled binary in IDA — tracing how a global pointer leads to an object, which leads
      to a vtable, which leads to a function. Finding <code>NAME_CHAIN = [0x17C, 0xA0, 0x18,
      0x34, 0x310]</code> in Cheat Engine required understanding what each dereference landed in.
      That's the same reasoning process a researcher applies when following a pointer chain in
      a disassembly listing.</p>

    <h3>Dynamic Analysis with a Known-Value Scan</h3>
    <p>The Cheat Engine methodology — known value, change, narrow, repeat — is a specific
      instance of a broader technique: using known program outputs to locate the internal state
      that produced them. In exploit research this looks like: allocate a controlled-size buffer,
      locate it in the heap, establish its offset from a disclosure gadget. In firmware analysis
      it looks like: send a known command, search the firmware's memory for the response buffer,
      trace backward to find the processing function. The game just provides the most immediate
      feedback loop. The <code>cmp dword ptr [esi+00000158], 02</code> instruction that confirmed
      the teamNum offset is the exact same read that a debugger would catch when breaking on a
      comparison in a binary you're reversing — the disassembly view, the register state, the
      confirmation that this instruction is the one the game actually uses.</p>

    <h3>WriteProcessMemory and Process Injection</h3>
    <p>The aimbot writes floats to view angle addresses. The radar writes integers to entity flag
      fields. The Windows API call for both is <code>WriteProcessMemory</code>. This is
      structurally identical to the write step in shellcode injection: allocate memory in the
      target process with <code>VirtualAllocEx</code>, write the payload with
      <code>WriteProcessMemory</code>, execute it with <code>CreateRemoteThread</code>. Building
      something that exercises the write primitive makes the mechanism real before approaching
      the more constrained injection scenarios in security research.</p>

    <h3>Anti-Cheat as the Defender</h3>
    <p>The most direct security research connection is the one between this project and the GPU
      passthrough project. VAC, EAC, and Vanguard are doing the same thing this tool does, from
      the other direction: scanning for unauthorised <code>ReadProcessMemory</code> calls to
      game memory, looking for injected modules in the process's module list, monitoring write
      frequency to protected addresses, checking for memory regions with RWX permissions that
      don't correspond to any loaded DLL.</p>
    <p>Understanding what the attacker can do is prerequisite to understanding what the defender
      must detect. You cannot reason clearly about anti-cheat evasion (as in the VFIO project)
      without understanding what anti-cheat is actually measuring. You cannot build a memory
      protection system without having built something that bypasses naive memory protection.
      This is the red-team/blue-team knowledge cycle that security research runs on.</p>

    <h3>The Mental Model It Forces</h3>
    <p>The most lasting output of this project is not the ESP or the aimbot. It's the internalised
      understanding that a running process's internal state is not private. Any process with
      appropriate handle rights can read or modify it. The only enforcement is the OS permission
      model (token privileges, handle access rights, protected process levels) and the software
      running in an equal or higher privilege ring to detect abuse. Kernel-mode anti-cheat like
      Vanguard exists precisely because userspace detection is bypassable with userspace tools
      — the same tools this project is built with.</p>
    <p>That framing — understanding the full permission model and its limits, at the API level —
      is the prerequisite mental model for Windows internals security research. It doesn't come
      from reading about it. It comes from building something that demonstrates it. The C++
      prototype being flagged by Defender before it ran, the pointer scan producing thousands of
      false candidates before narrowing to one, the VEH debugger fix required to get past the
      access violation — none of those are in a textbook. They're what actually happens when
      you do the work.</p>

    <!-- ============================================================ -->
    <h2>Current State &amp; What Comes Next</h2>

    <p>The tool is a research artefact built specifically against <strong>CS:CO (Counter-Strike:
      Classic Offensive)</strong> — a community mod that blends CS:GO, Counter-Strike: Source, and
      CS 1.6 into a single client. That hybrid lineage is what makes it a more interesting target
      than vanilla CS:GO: the memory layout borrows from several engine generations, so the offsets
      don't cleanly match any published CS:GO or CSS offset list. Every address in the constants
      block was sourced independently against this specific build — which is also what makes it a
      better learning exercise, because there is no offset dump to copy from.</p>
    <p>The natural next step in the same direction is kernel-level work. All of the techniques
      here operate in userspace, which means they're visible to any kernel-mode monitor. A
      <code>ReadProcessMemory</code> call from userspace is observable via kernel callbacks
      (<code>PsSetLoadImageNotifyRoutine</code>, ETW providers, minifilter drivers). Understanding
      what moves to ring-0 to become undetectable is the same question the GPU passthrough
      project approaches from the anti-cheat evasion angle — and the same question that leads
      into driver development, IOCTL analysis, and kernel vulnerability research.</p>

    <div class="note"><p><strong>Note.</strong> Built on hardware and a game licence I own,
      as a personal research project for understanding Windows process memory, the Win32 security
      model, and the technical basis of game anti-cheat. Not used in online multiplayer against
      other players.</p></div>
  </article>

